return [
    11 => [
        'overview' => 'Marketplace Seller Central Professional Certification is the complete program for serious marketplace sellers. It covers strategy and positioning, catalog excellence, pricing and margin discipline, fulfilment and logistics, customer service, payments and disputes, and data-driven growth. Finish the program to earn your professional seller certification.',
        'duration' => 420,
        'lessons' => [
            [
                'title' => 'Marketplace Strategy and Store Positioning',
                'summary' => 'Decide who you sell to and why they choose you.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "A professional seller starts with strategy, not listings. Strategy is the answer to three questions: who is my buyer, what do I sell them, and why do they choose me over the other sellers they could find? Position without answers to these is just noise in the marketplace.\n\nBegin with your buyer. Are they local buyers seeking fresh produce, processors buying in bulk, or buyers in distant cities needing reliable delivery? Each buyer cares about different things: freshness and grade for local buyers, capacity and consistency for processors, lead time and packaging for distant ones. Your store should speak to one of these clearly.\n\nPosition around a real strength. Maybe your farm produces a superior grade, maybe your location gives you the freshest supply in the region, maybe your logistics can deliver where others cannot. Choose the strength you can actually deliver every week, and build the storefront story around it. A narrow, true position beats a broad, false one.\n\nStudy the competitive shelf. Review comparable listings for your product and grade: their titles, prices, stated capacity, and reviews. Gaps in the market show up as products nobody lists well, or buyers' complaints nobody answers. Positioning in a gap is easier than fighting head-on on price.\n\nFinally, commit to a standard: the grade you will supply, the capacity you can sustain, the service level you will deliver. Write it down and treat it as a contract with every buyer. Strategy is a promise you can keep; the storefront is where you keep it, in public, every single week.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Catalog Management and Listing Excellence',
                'summary' => 'Build a catalog that is findable, precise, and trustworthy.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "Your catalog is your shop's inventory of trust. Each listing is a promise about a product, and buyers judge your professionalism by the precision and consistency of those promises. Catalog management is the discipline of keeping every promise accurate.\n\nStructure the catalog for search. Buyers filter by category, product, grade, and location, so each listing must sit in the right category with a title that names the product, its grade or form, and the unit. A mis-categorized or vaguely titled listing is invisible to the buyers who want it most.\n\nMake listings precise and verifiable. State the grade, the form (fresh, husked, de-husked, copra), the unit of measure, and the package or bulk arrangement. Include a photo where you can, taken at your own farm or depot; genuine photos build more trust than any claim. Describe what is included honestly, including defects or seasonal variation.\n\nKeep the catalog current. Update prices when the market moves, mark items out of stock the moment they sell out, and refresh listings when the harvest changes the available grade. A stale catalog misleads buyers and generates cancellations, which damage your reliability score and your visibility.\n\nFinally, review the catalog monthly as a buyer would. Search for your own products, compare your listings to the best sellers', and fix the weakest ones. The seller with a precise, current, honest catalog wins the buyer's trust before the first message is ever sent.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Pricing, Margin and Cost Discipline',
                'summary' => 'Price for profit, not for the loudest inquiry.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Pricing is where sellers make or lose their business. The professional seller prices for profit with full knowledge of cost, rather than reacting to every inquiry or matching the cheapest listing. Margin discipline is the difference between a busy shop and a profitable one.\n\nKnow your true cost per unit. For produce, that means farm costs, harvesting, sorting, packaging, transport, and marketplace fees, divided by the quantity you can actually sell. Add the cost of waste: nuts that crack, spoil, or are downgraded are part of the real cost. Only with true cost can you set a floor price below which a sale is a loss.\n\nPrice against the market, informed by your cost. Check comparable listings for the same grade and form, and position deliberately: premium pricing for a premium, verifiable grade; competitive pricing for volume. Never bait with a low price you intend to negotiate upward; the marketplace penalizes it, and buyers abandon sellers who waste their time.\n\nProtect margin at the point of sale. Confirm quantity, grade, delivery terms, and price in writing before committing. Watch for hidden costs such as loading, transport, and payment fees that quietly erode margin; quote them explicitly or absorb them knowingly, never accidentally.\n\nFinally, review margins monthly by product. Some listings will be your profit drivers and others your time-wasters; the data tells you which to push and which to drop. The seller who knows their numbers sets prices that win business and pay the farm.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Order Fulfilment and Logistics',
                'summary' => 'Deliver the right product, on time, in the right condition.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Fulfilment is the moment the listing's promise becomes real. Buyers judge sellers by what arrives at their door: the right product, the agreed grade and quantity, on time, in good condition. Logistics discipline is what makes that happen repeatedly.\n\nFulfil from a written order. Confirm the product, grade, quantity, price, and delivery terms before picking anything. For fresh produce, agree on quality at dispatch and photograph the consignment before handover; the photo is your evidence if the arrival is disputed.\n\nPlan logistics realistically. Know your lead times by distance, and quote them honestly rather than promising speed you cannot deliver. For fresh produce, time the harvest to the order where possible, so the buyer receives the freshest possible product rather than day-old stock.\n\nHandle transport with care. Use clean, dry vehicles; keep nuts out of direct sun and rain; and avoid overloading, which crushes and cracks. For bulk orders, weigh and count at dispatch and record the figures; a documented dispatch settles most quantity disputes in seconds.\n\nClose the loop at delivery. Confirm the buyer received the agreed grade and quantity, and resolve any shortfall immediately rather than after a complaint. Track your fulfilment metrics, on-time rate and damage rate, because they are the numbers that determine your marketplace ranking and your repeat business.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Customer Service and Reputation Management',
                'summary' => 'Turn buyers into repeat customers and advocates.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Reputation is the compounding asset of marketplace selling. It is built one answered message and one fulfilled order at a time, and it is destroyed by one ignored inquiry or one defensive argument. Customer service is the discipline of earning it deliberately.\n\nRespond fast and completely. Reply to inquiries within a day, answer the specific questions asked, and confirm anything you promise in writing inside the platform. The message history is your evidence, and a seller who replies promptly ranks higher and converts better.\n\nManage expectations before the sale. State the grade, quantity, lead time, and delivery terms clearly, and do not oversell what you cannot supply. A buyer who knows what to expect is a satisfied buyer; a buyer surprised by reality is a dispute, whatever the product quality.\n\nHandle problems with ownership. If something goes wrong, tell the buyer immediately, explain the cause, and propose a remedy: replacement, partial refund, or a discount on the next order. Early ownership defuses most complaints; defensiveness escalates them.\n\nFinally, ask for feedback and read it. Positive reviews are your marketing; negative ones are your free consulting. The seller who thanks reviewers, fixes the causes, and shows improvement over time builds a reputation that outlasts any single listing or season.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Payments, Disputes and Refund Handling',
                'summary' => 'Keep money flows clean and disputes rare and fair.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Marketplace payments protect both sides: the buyer's payment is held until they confirm receipt, and the seller is paid once the order is confirmed. Understanding this flow, and handling the exceptions fairly, keeps your money clean and your disputes few.\n\nWork inside the platform payment flow. Never ask buyers to pay outside the platform: off-platform payments remove your protection, make disputes nearly impossible to resolve, and violate marketplace policy. The hold-and-release flow is your guarantee that an honest seller gets paid for an honest delivery.\n\nTrack payments and holds in your own records. Match each order to its payment status, and reconcile weekly against your wallet transactions. Pending holds are normal; a hold that never resolves, or a payment that never appears, needs a support ticket with the transaction reference.\n\nHandle disputes and refunds by policy and evidence. When a buyer raises a quality or quantity issue, review the dispatch photos and records first, respond promptly, and offer a fair remedy. For refunds, follow the platform process; refunds return to the original payment method, and wallet refunds are faster than card refunds.\n\nFinally, learn from every dispute. Each one has a root cause, in the product, the listing, the packaging, or the logistics. The seller who fixes root causes, rather than just settling cases, watches their dispute rate fall and their ranking rise.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Growth: Analytics, Seasonality and Scaling',
                'summary' => 'Grow with data, season by season.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Growth in a marketplace is a data problem. The sellers who scale are the ones who read their numbers, respect the seasonality of their product, and expand capacity only as fast as quality allows. Growth without that discipline is a path to cancelled orders and a broken reputation.\n\nStart with the metrics that matter: views, inquiries, conversion rate, repeat rate, fulfilment rate, and average rating. These tell you where buyers drop off and what keeps them. A listing with many views but few inquiries has a title, price, or photo problem; a listing with inquiries but no orders has a trust or price problem.\n\nPlan for seasonality. Coconut supply, price, and quality vary with the season, and so does demand. Track your own sales by month, plan stock and pricing around the calendar, and use off-seasons to strengthen the farm, the catalog, and the team. Sellers who plan ahead of the season avoid the panic-buying mistakes of the peak.\n\nScale in steps, not leaps. Add capacity, staff, or a new product line only when the current operation is reliably excellent. Each expansion should preserve your quality and fulfilment standards; growth that breaks those standards costs more in reputation than it earns in sales.\n\nFinally, diversify intelligently: new grades, new forms of the same product, or new buyer segments you already serve well. Data-driven, seasonal, step-by-step growth is how a professional seller builds a business the marketplace rewards.",
            ],
        ],
        'materials' => [
            ['title' => 'Seller Strategy Canvas', 'type' => 'template', 'notes' => 'Buyer, product, position, and delivery standard.'],
            ['title' => 'Listing Quality Rubric', 'type' => 'reference', 'notes' => 'Category, title, grade, unit, photo, and honesty standards.'],
            ['title' => 'Cost and Margin Calculator', 'type' => 'template', 'notes' => 'True cost per unit, floor price, and margin targets.'],
            ['title' => 'Fulfilment and Dispatch Checklist', 'type' => 'checklist', 'notes' => 'Order confirmation, dispatch photos, weighing, and handover.'],
        ],
        'assessment' => [
            'title' => 'Marketplace Seller Central Professional Assessment',
            'instructions' => 'Answer all questions. A score of 70% or higher is required to pass. You have up to 3 attempts.',
            'pass_score' => 70,
            'questions' => [
                ['question' => 'What three questions define a seller\'s strategy?', 'a' => 'Price, stock, and advertising budget', 'b' => 'Who the buyer is, what to sell them, and why they choose you', 'c' => 'How many listings, how many photos, and how many discounts', 'd' => 'Which competitors to copy, and how fast', 'correct' => 'B'],
                ['question' => 'Why should a seller know their true cost per unit?', 'a' => 'To set a floor price below which a sale is a loss', 'b' => 'To impress buyers with arithmetic', 'c' => 'Because the marketplace requires it for listing', 'd' => 'To justify the highest price in the market', 'correct' => 'A'],
                ['question' => 'What is the correct way to handle a buyer payment request outside the platform?', 'a' => 'Accept it if the buyer insists', 'b' => 'Decline politely and use the platform checkout', 'c' => 'Accept but keep a screenshot', 'd' => 'Raise the price for off-platform payment', 'correct' => 'B'],
                ['question' => 'A listing has many views but few inquiries. What is the likely problem?', 'a' => 'The title, price, or photo is weak', 'b' => 'The product is too good', 'c' => 'The marketplace is broken', 'd' => 'The seller is answering too fast', 'correct' => 'A'],
                ['question' => 'What is the safest approach to scaling a marketplace business?', 'a' => 'Expand as fast as possible to capture demand', 'b' => 'Grow in steps that preserve quality and fulfilment standards', 'c' => 'Add products before testing the first one', 'd' => 'Cut prices to zero margin to win volume', 'correct' => 'B'],
            ],
        ],
    ],

    12 => [
        'overview' => 'State Coordinator Operations Intelligence trains state coordinators to run their state as an evidence-based operation. It covers the coordinator operating model, coverage and field force management, dashboards and data intelligence, verification quality, program delivery, risk and escalation, and performance reviews that drive continuous improvement.',
        'duration' => 420,
        'lessons' => [
            [
                'title' => 'The State Coordinator Operating Model',
                'summary' => 'What the coordinator owns, and how the state runs.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "A state coordinator is the accountable operator of the platform's presence in their state: the grower base, the field agents, the providers, and the programs that reach them. The operating model defines what you own, what you measure, and how your team works together.\n\nStart with your mandate. You are responsible for the quality and growth of every registry record, verification, and program in your state. That means targets for verified growers, active providers, field coverage, and program participation, all reported through the platform's dashboards. Clarify the mandate with your supervisor and write it down.\n\nUnderstand the team structure. Field agents execute verifications and visits; providers supply inputs and services; growers are the core constituency. Your job is not to do the agents' visits but to make sure every visit is evidenced, accurate, and on schedule. You manage through data and reviews, not through doing the work yourself.\n\nMap the state's coverage. Know the LGAs where you have strong presence, the ones you barely reach, and why. Coverage gaps are where unregistered growers, unverified farms, and informal providers hide. A coverage map with a plan for each gap is your operating blueprint.\n\nFinally, run a rhythm: a weekly operations review with your agents, a monthly state review with the numbers, and a quarterly planning session. The coordinator who runs a rhythm keeps the state moving; the one who reacts to the loudest problem loses the week to fire-fighting.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Coverage and Field Force Management',
                'summary' => 'Deploy agents where the state needs them most.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Coverage is the reach of your operation: which LGAs and farms your field force actually serves. Field force management is the discipline of deploying agents so that coverage is complete, visits are scheduled, and no grower is invisible to the platform.\n\nMeasure coverage honestly. Count verified growers, active farms, and providers per LGA, and divide by the estimated totals to see the real percentage. Dashboards show the numbers, but the coordinator's job is to explain them: why one LGA is at ninety percent and another at ten.\n\nDeploy agents against the gaps, not the geography. An agent assigned to a well-covered LGA adds little; the same agent in an underserved LGA can transform its numbers. Build assignment plans that target the weakest coverage first, and set clear targets per agent per month.\n\nSchedule and track visits. Agents should plan their week with a visit list, capture GPS and photo evidence at each visit, and submit records the same day. Your review of those records, within a day or two, is what keeps evidence quality high and visits honest.\n\nFinally, balance workload and travel. Long travel times eat productive hours, so cluster visits by area. A field force that is well-deployed, well-scheduled, and well-supervised is what turns a state's coverage map from ambition into fact.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Data Intelligence and Dashboards',
                'summary' => 'Read dashboards, spot signals, and act on them.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "The platform's dashboards turn the state's activity into intelligence. The coordinator's skill is reading them well: knowing which numbers matter, what a change in them means, and which signal deserves action this week.\n\nLearn the core dashboards: registry growth, verification quality, coverage, program participation, and field activity. For each, know the definition exactly, because a misunderstood metric leads to wrong conclusions. Registry growth means new verified records, not new signups; the distinction matters.\n\nRead trends, not just levels. A single week's number is noise; four weeks of decline is a signal. Compare your state against its own history and against peer states, and ask what changed when a trend turned: a new agent, a seasonal effect, a program launch, or a data problem.\n\nDrill into anomalies before acting. A spike in rejections, a cluster of failed verifications, or a sudden drop in one LGA always has a cause. Investigate with the data and the agents before concluding anything; the dashboard points, the field explains.\n\nFinally, run the state's data quality. Duplicates, unverified phones, and stale records distort every dashboard. A monthly data-quality pass, clearing duplicates and prompting verification updates, keeps the intelligence honest. The coordinator who trusts clean data makes better decisions than the one who trusts instinct.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Verification Quality and Sampling',
                'summary' => 'Keep field verifications accurate, honest, and complete.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Verification is the product the whole platform sells to buyers and programs: the claim that a grower, farm, or provider is real and accurately described. As coordinator, you own the quality of that product in your state.\n\nDefine what a good verification looks like: identity confirmed, GPS evidence at the farm's identifiable point, a complete photo set, declared facts checked, and a same-day submission. Share that definition with every agent, and use it as the review standard.\n\nSample verifications weekly. Pick a small random sample of each agent's submissions, and review the evidence as if you were the verifier: does the GPS point match the landmark photo, do the photos show the declared farm, is anything missing? Sampling catches drift early, before it becomes habit.\n\nWatch for the failure patterns. Weak GPS fixes, generic photos, missing captions, and copy-paste notes are the usual signs of rushed work. Address them with coaching, not blame: show the agent the standard, the gap, and the fix. Repeat problems after coaching are a performance issue, not a training one.\n\nFinally, protect integrity absolutely. Any sign of falsified evidence, ghost farms, or collusion with a grower is a serious breach: suspend the record, preserve the evidence, and escalate immediately. Verification quality is the platform's currency; the coordinator is its guardian in the state.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Program Delivery and Campaign Execution',
                'summary' => 'Deliver programs that reach the intended growers.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Programs, input campaigns, and training initiatives only work if they reach the growers they were designed for. Program delivery is the coordinator's discipline of translating a national plan into a state's executed reality.\n\nStart by understanding the program's intent: who it targets, what it provides, and what success measures. A program for young palms needs a different grower list than a harvest-input campaign; targeting the wrong growers wastes resources and damages trust in every future program.\n\nBuild the delivery plan: the LGAs, the agents, the timeline, and the communication to growers. Use the registry to build the eligible list, verify contact details, and brief the field force. A program that launches without a communication plan reaches only the growers who already know about it.\n\nExecute and monitor. Track reach against the target list daily during the campaign: how many eligible growers were contacted, how many enrolled, how many received the benefit. Gaps between plan and reality are your early warning; close them while the campaign is still live, not after.\n\nFinally, report outcomes honestly. Programs are evaluated on real results, and inflated reporting poisons the next program's design. Record what actually happened, including shortfalls and the reasons. The coordinator who delivers and reports honestly earns the right to ask for more programs for their state.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Risk, Escalation and Incident Management',
                'summary' => 'See risks early and escalate what must rise.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "State operations carry real risks: verification fraud, safety incidents, data breaches, weather events, and community complaints. Risk management is the discipline of seeing them early and handling them at the right level, quickly.\n\nMaintain a risk list for the state. Walk through the operations and write down what could go wrong: an agent falsifying evidence, a farm safety incident, a data leak, a storm destroying a program's target area. For each, note the likelihood, the impact, and the early warning sign.\n\nEscalate by the book. Every platform has escalation paths for fraud, safety, and data incidents. Learn them, and escalate the moment the trigger is met, with the facts and evidence attached. Escalation is not failure; delayed escalation of a fraud or a safety incident is. The coordinator who escalates early protects the grower, the agent, and the program.\n\nHandle incidents with care. For a safety incident, the priorities are people first, then evidence, then process: ensure medical care, preserve the record, and report. For a data incident, preserve access logs and notify through the security path immediately.\n\nFinally, review incidents and near-misses monthly. Most serious failures were visible as near-misses first. The coordinator who reads near-misses as free lessons builds a state operation that gets safer and more resilient every quarter.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Performance Reviews and Continuous Improvement',
                'summary' => 'Review, coach, and improve the state operation.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Performance management is what turns a state's activity into a state's results. The coordinator runs a review cadence that measures the team, coaches individuals, and improves the operation month after month.\n\nStructure the reviews in layers. Weekly, review agent activity with each agent: visits completed, evidence quality, and blockers. Monthly, review the state's numbers against targets: verification quality, coverage, program reach, and data quality. Quarterly, review the strategy: which gaps closed, which remain, and what changes.\n\nCoach with evidence, not opinion. Bring the specific record, the specific gap, and the standard to every coaching conversation. 'Three of last week's visits lacked a landmark photo; here is the standard and here is last month's good example' is coaching that works; 'your work is sloppy' is not.\n\nRecognize and retain good performers. Agents who deliver high-quality verifications consistently are the backbone of the state; acknowledge their work publicly and give them the harder assignments. Retention of good agents is a coordinator's key result, because hiring and training a new agent costs months of productivity.\n\nFinally, improve the process, not just the people. When a problem recurs across agents, the process is the culprit: unclear standards, bad tools, or unrealistic targets. Fix the process once, and the whole team benefits. That is continuous improvement at state scale.",
            ],
        ],
        'materials' => [
            ['title' => 'State Operations Dashboard Guide', 'type' => 'guide', 'notes' => 'Core dashboards, definitions, and trend reading.'],
            ['title' => 'Coverage Planning Template', 'type' => 'template', 'notes' => 'LGA coverage, gaps, agent assignments, and targets.'],
            ['title' => 'Verification Sampling Sheet', 'type' => 'template', 'notes' => 'Weekly sample review against the verification standard.'],
            ['title' => 'State Risk Register', 'type' => 'template', 'notes' => 'Likelihood, impact, early warning, and escalation path.'],
        ],
        'assessment' => [
            'title' => 'State Coordinator Operations Intelligence Assessment',
            'instructions' => 'Answer all questions. A score of 70% or higher is required to pass. You have up to 3 attempts.',
            'pass_score' => 70,
            'questions' => [
                ['question' => 'What is the coordinator\'s primary management tool?', 'a' => 'Doing the agents\' visits personally', 'b' => 'Data, dashboards, and a review rhythm', 'c' => 'Weekly meetings with every grower', 'd' => 'Price negotiations with providers', 'correct' => 'B'],
                ['question' => 'How should agents be deployed to improve coverage?', 'a' => 'Evenly across all LGAs regardless of need', 'b' => 'Against the weakest-coverage LGAs first', 'c' => 'Only to LGAs with the most complaints', 'd' => 'Wherever travel is cheapest', 'correct' => 'B'],
                ['question' => 'What should a coordinator do when a trend turns downward for several weeks?', 'a' => 'Wait for the annual review to act', 'b' => 'Drill into the anomaly, investigate the cause, and act', 'c' => 'Adjust the dashboard to look better', 'd' => 'Reassign the agent responsible immediately', 'correct' => 'B'],
                ['question' => 'What is the correct response to suspected falsified verification evidence?', 'a' => 'Coach the agent and move on', 'b' => 'Ignore it unless a buyer complains', 'c' => 'Suspend the record, preserve evidence, and escalate immediately', 'd' => 'Delete the record quietly', 'correct' => 'C'],
                ['question' => 'Why review near-misses monthly?', 'a' => 'They are required paperwork for audits', 'b' => 'Most serious failures were visible as near-misses first', 'c' => 'They increase the state\'s reported numbers', 'd' => 'They replace the need for a risk register', 'correct' => 'B'],
            ],
        ],
    ],

    13 => [
        'overview' => 'National Coordinator Impact and Reporting Intelligence trains national coordinators to run impact measurement and reporting across the platform. It covers operations frameworks, reporting architecture, KPIs and targets, state benchmarking, outcome measurement, stakeholder and executive reporting, and data governance for decision intelligence.',
        'duration' => 420,
        'lessons' => [
            [
                'title' => 'National Operations and Impact Frameworks',
                'summary' => 'Connect operations to outcomes at national scale.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "At national scale, the coordinator's job is to see the whole system: how operations in every state combine into outcomes for growers, buyers, and the cooperative. An impact framework is the map that connects activity to outcome, and it is the first tool of national intelligence.\n\nStart with the theory of change. What activities does the platform run, what do they produce, and who benefits? Field verifications produce trusted records; trusted records enable programs, financing, and market access; market access raises grower income. Each arrow in that chain is a hypothesis the coordinator tests with data.\n\nDefine the outcome areas: grower verification and inclusion, farm productivity, market access, and program integrity. For each, name the outcomes you expect and the indicators that measure them. National coordination without named outcomes is travel and meetings; with them, it is progress you can prove.\n\nUnderstand the operating layers: state coordinators run operations, the platform runs the systems, and programs run the interventions. The national coordinator aligns these layers, ensuring state activity feeds the systems and the programs reinforce the operations.\n\nFinally, treat the framework as a living document. Review it quarterly against real data, and revise the hypotheses that the numbers disprove. The framework that evolves with evidence is the one that keeps national strategy honest.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Reporting Architecture and Data Standards',
                'summary' => 'Design the flow of numbers from farm to board.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Reporting at national scale is an architecture problem: numbers flow from farms and field visits, through state operations, into national dashboards, and out to stakeholders. The architecture must be consistent, timely, and trustworthy, or every decision built on it is built on sand.\n\nSet data standards first. Every record, verification, and program report should carry the same definitions: what counts as a verified grower, an active provider, a completed visit, a program reach. When states measure differently, the national totals are fiction. Standards are the grammar of reporting.\n\nDesign the reporting cadence: weekly field activity, monthly state summaries, quarterly program reviews, and annual impact reporting. Each layer serves a different audience, from operations teams to executives to donors, and each should be produced from the same underlying data, not rebuilt by hand.\n\nAutomate before you add people. The dashboards should aggregate from the operational systems, so the reporting team spends time interpreting rather than copying numbers. Manual reconciliation across states is slow, error-prone, and unauditable at scale.\n\nFinally, publish a data dictionary and a reporting calendar. When every team knows the definitions and the deadlines, reporting becomes a rhythm rather than a monthly scramble, and the numbers gain the credibility that good decisions require.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'KPIs, Targets and Performance Management',
                'summary' => 'Choose the numbers that steer the platform.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Key performance indicators steer a large organisation: teams do what the numbers reward. Choosing the right KPIs, setting sensible targets, and managing performance against them is the national coordinator's core leverage.\n\nChoose KPIs that reflect outcomes, not just activity. Verification count is activity; verified-grower quality and program eligibility unlocked are outcomes. Sales of inputs is activity; adoption of recommended practice and yield change are outcomes. A KPI set that rewards activity alone will produce busy teams and flat results.\n\nSet targets with context. A flat national target ignores that states differ in size, maturity, and coverage. Benchmark targets on each state's baseline and potential, and review them quarterly. Targets that are impossible demoralise; targets that are trivial waste the year.\n\nManage performance through the cadence. Monthly, compare each state against its targets and its own trend; investigate the outliers on both ends, the laggards and the standouts. The standouts hold lessons for the laggards, and the laggards reveal systemic blockers only a national view can remove.\n\nFinally, guard against KPI gaming. Any number that becomes a target attracts manipulation, so pair each KPI with a quality check: verification counts with sampling quality, reach with beneficiary confirmation. The platform that measures well performs well.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'State Performance Review and Benchmarking',
                'summary' => 'Compare states fairly and learn from the leaders.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "State performance review is where national intelligence becomes action. The art is comparing states fairly, learning from the leaders, and lifting the laggards without punishing honest reporting.\n\nBenchmark with like-for-like groups. Compare states with similar size, maturity, and coverage, not a single national league table. A small, remote state should be measured against its own potential and trajectory, or the comparison punishes geography rather than rewarding performance.\n\nRead performance in layers. The headline number is the outcome; the middle layer is the operational drivers, such as verification quality and agent activity; the base layer is data quality. A state that looks weak may have a data problem rather than a performance problem, and the fix differs completely.\n\nStudy the leaders deliberately. When a state outperforms, send a review team to understand why: a better coverage plan, a stronger review rhythm, a creative program delivery. Turn the leader's practice into a playbook, and spread it to peer states. That is how a national organisation learns at scale.\n\nFinally, protect honest reporting. If missing targets triggers punishment, states will massage numbers and the national picture becomes fiction. Review performance to diagnose and support, not to blame. The coordinator who builds a blame-free review culture gets the truth, and the truth is the only basis for improvement.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Impact Measurement and Outcome Reporting',
                'summary' => 'Measure what changed for growers, not just what was done.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Impact measurement answers the question donors, executives, and growers all ask: what actually changed? Outputs count activities; outcomes describe change. The national coordinator must measure outcomes honestly, even when the change is slow or the results are modest.\n\nDefine outcome indicators for each program: yield change for farm programs, income change for market programs, verification quality for registry programs. Where possible, measure against a baseline recorded before the program started; without a baseline, impact is a claim, not a measurement.\n\nUse mixed evidence. Dashboards show the quantities, but outcomes often need qualitative evidence: grower testimonies, field observations, and sample interviews. Triangulate the quantitative and the qualitative; two independent sources agreeing is far stronger than either alone.\n\nAttribute carefully. A yield rise during a program year may be weather, price, or the program itself. State the limits of attribution honestly, and use comparison where you can, such as participating versus similar non-participating growers. Overclaiming impact destroys the credibility of future reporting.\n\nFinally, report outcomes on a rhythm and learn from them. Programs that show weak outcomes need redesign, not louder promotion. The organisation that measures outcomes honestly, including the disappointments, improves faster than the one that only celebrates.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Stakeholder, Donor and Executive Reporting',
                'summary' => 'Tell the story the numbers support, to each audience.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Reporting is communication, and different audiences need different stories from the same data. Executives need decisions, donors need results against their funding, and field teams need what to do next. Tailoring the report to the audience is the skill that makes reporting useful.\n\nFor executives, lead with the decision. State the headline result, the trend, the risk, and the choice you need, supported by two or three charts. An executive report that buries the ask in a deck of tables gets no decision.\n\nFor donors, report against their framework: the activities funded, the outputs delivered, the outcomes measured, and the evidence behind each. Be transparent about shortfalls and the corrective plan; donors fund honesty far longer than they fund perfection.\n\nFor field teams, translate the numbers into actions: which states need coverage, which programs need delivery push, which evidence standards slipped. A report that field staff cannot act on is a filing exercise.\n\nFinally, keep one version of the truth. All these reports should come from the same underlying data and definitions, so no audience ever hears a number that contradicts another. Consistency across audiences is what builds the platform's credibility over years.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Data Governance and Decision Intelligence',
                'summary' => 'Trustworthy data, governed well, drives better decisions.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Data governance is the set of rules that keeps the platform's data trustworthy: who owns each dataset, who may change it, how quality is enforced, and how sensitive data is protected. At national scale, governance is not bureaucracy; it is the foundation of every decision.\n\nAssign data ownership. Every key dataset, from the registry to program records, needs an accountable owner who defines the standard and enforces it. Data without an owner decays: duplicates multiply, definitions drift, and dashboards quietly lose meaning.\n\nEnforce quality at the source. Validation rules at entry, such as state and LGA dropdowns, phone formats, and duplicate checks, prevent errors cheaper than any cleanup campaign. Governance that starts at the source keeps the national picture clean without heroic effort.\n\nProtect sensitive data. Grower identities, phone numbers, and transaction records are sensitive. Limit access to those who need it for their role, audit access, and train every team on the handling rules. A single data leak can undo years of grower trust.\n\nFinally, use governance to enable decisions, not to block them. The goal is that any team, at any level, can trust the number in front of them. The organisation with governed, trusted data makes faster, better decisions than the one with more data and less trust.",
            ],
        ],
        'materials' => [
            ['title' => 'Impact Framework Canvas', 'type' => 'template', 'notes' => 'Theory of change, outcome areas, and indicators.'],
            ['title' => 'National Reporting Calendar', 'type' => 'template', 'notes' => 'Cadence, owners, audiences, and deadlines.'],
            ['title' => 'KPI Dictionary', 'type' => 'reference', 'notes' => 'Definitions, formulas, targets, and quality checks.'],
            ['title' => 'State Benchmarking Sheet', 'type' => 'template', 'notes' => 'Like-for-like groups, drivers, and leader playbooks.'],
        ],
        'assessment' => [
            'title' => 'National Coordinator Impact and Reporting Assessment',
            'instructions' => 'Answer all questions. A score of 70% or higher is required to pass. You have up to 3 attempts.',
            'pass_score' => 70,
            'questions' => [
                ['question' => 'What does an impact framework primarily connect?', 'a' => 'Travel plans to meeting schedules', 'b' => 'Activities to outcomes through a testable chain', 'c' => 'Donor names to report covers', 'd' => 'State budgets to office sizes', 'correct' => 'B'],
                ['question' => 'Why are data standards essential before national reporting?', 'a' => 'They make reports longer and more impressive', 'b' => 'Without common definitions, national totals are fiction', 'c' => 'Donors require a fixed font size', 'd' => 'They reduce the number of dashboards needed', 'correct' => 'B'],
                ['question' => 'What is the risk of setting only activity-based KPIs?', 'a' => 'Teams become busy while outcomes stay flat', 'b' => 'Dashboards become too colourful', 'c' => 'States will refuse to report', 'd' => 'It makes audits impossible to schedule', 'correct' => 'A'],
                ['question' => 'How should states be benchmarked fairly?', 'a' => 'In a single national league table', 'b' => 'In like-for-like groups by size, maturity, and coverage', 'c' => 'Only against the best-performing state', 'd' => 'Only against last year\'s weather', 'correct' => 'B'],
                ['question' => 'Why must impact attribution be stated honestly?', 'a' => 'Overclaiming destroys the credibility of future reporting', 'b' => 'Donors prefer shorter reports', 'c' => 'It reduces the reported budget', 'd' => 'It is a legal requirement for all cooperatives', 'correct' => 'A'],
            ],
        ],
    ],

    14 => [
        'overview' => 'NATCODEV Super Admin Governance and RBAC Masterclass is the platform\'s highest-level administration program. It covers governance and the admin mandate, role-based access control design, user lifecycle and session security, configuration and change control, audit trails, incident response, and governance metrics. This masterclass is required for platform super administrators.',
        'duration' => 420,
        'lessons' => [
            [
                'title' => 'Platform Governance and the Admin Mandate',
                'summary' => 'What super admins own, and the limits of the power.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "A super administrator holds the platform's highest access: the ability to configure systems, manage roles, and reach any data. Governance is the discipline that turns that power into responsible stewardship, and it starts with understanding what the mandate actually is.\n\nDefine the mandate explicitly. Super admins own the platform's configuration, its role model, and its integrity: not the day-to-day content, disputes, or field operations. When a super admin spends their week doing coordinator work, the platform is under-governed and over-operated. Write the mandate down and hold yourself to it.\n\nUnderstand the separation of duties. No single person should be able to create a role, assign it to themselves, and approve a sensitive change unnoticed. Where the platform allows it, require a second approver for high-risk actions. Separation of duties is what makes even a super admin's work reviewable.\n\nRespect the data. Super access can read grower identities, wallet transactions, and support conversations; that access is a responsibility, not a perk. View sensitive data only when the job requires it, and never use it for anything outside the platform's purpose.\n\nFinally, document your decisions. Every configuration change, role creation, and policy exception should have a reason recorded. The governance habit of documenting turns individual actions into institutional memory, and it is what an audit, an investigation, or your successor will rely on.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'RBAC Design: Roles, Permissions and Boundaries',
                'summary' => 'Design a role model that is least-privilege and reviewable.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Role-based access control is the platform's contract with its users: each role gets exactly the access its work needs, and nothing more. Good RBAC design is least-privilege by default, explicit in its boundaries, and cheap to review.\n\nStart from the jobs, not the people. Define roles around functions: grower, field agent, provider, coordinator, support agent, finance, auditor, admin. Each role's permissions should map to that function's tasks. When permissions follow jobs, access reviews become simple and departures become safe.\n\nApply least privilege. Give the minimum permissions that let the role do its work, and grant elevated permissions in time-boxed or approval-gated ways where possible. A field agent does not need wallet refund rights; a support agent does not need configuration access. Every extra permission is a risk you must justify.\n\nKeep the role model small and documented. A handful of well-defined roles with written purposes are manageable; dozens of bespoke roles are a security debt that nobody can audit. Name roles clearly, document what each may do, and version the model as it evolves.\n\nFinally, review access regularly. Quarterly, walk the role assignments: who holds each role, whether they still need it, and whether any assignment is surprising. The RBAC model that is reviewed stays aligned with the organisation; the one that is not quietly accumulates privilege.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'User Lifecycle, Authentication and Session Security',
                'summary' => 'Secure accounts from creation to deactivation.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Every access incident begins with an account. The user lifecycle, from creation to deactivation, is where authentication security is won: strong identities, strong authentication, and prompt removal when people leave.\n\nProvision accounts deliberately. Create accounts only for real, identified people, with the correct role from day one. Shared or generic accounts destroy accountability, because the audit trail cannot say who acted. If a process seems to need a shared account, the role model needs fixing instead.\n\nEnforce strong authentication. Require unique passwords, encourage or mandate two-factor authentication for privileged roles, and rate-limit login attempts to stop brute force. One-time passwords must be short-lived and single-use. Authentication is the gate that makes every other control meaningful.\n\nManage sessions carefully. Sessions should expire, be invalidated on logout, and be visible to users as active sessions they can revoke. When a device is lost or a user suspects compromise, revoking sessions is the fastest containment.\n\nDeactivate promptly. When someone leaves a role or the organisation, remove their access the same day. The most common access breach is not a sophisticated hack; it is a former employee whose account was never disabled. The lifecycle that ends on time is the lifecycle that keeps the platform safe.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Configuration, Maintenance and Change Control',
                'summary' => 'Change the platform safely and reversibly.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Configuration changes, from a new fee to a maintenance mode, affect every user at once. Change control is the discipline that makes those changes deliberate, tested, reversible, and documented, so a bad change becomes a short incident rather than a long outage.\n\nTreat configuration as controlled data. Every configuration value should have an owner, a purpose, and a documented range of valid values. Changes made by memory, in production, with no record are the root of most configuration outages.\n\nFollow a change routine. For any significant change: describe it, assess the risk, plan the rollback, schedule it in a low-traffic window, and communicate it to affected teams. Maintenance pages and banners exist for a reason; use them when a change could interrupt users.\n\nTest before you ship. Where the platform allows, stage or pilot the change: a new role to one team, a new fee to a test scope, a feature behind a flag. A change validated on a small scope is a change that rarely surprises the whole platform.\n\nFinally, keep a change log. Who changed what, when, and why is the first thing an incident review asks for. The change log turns firefighting into learning, and it turns a one-person knowledge base into an organisation's operational memory.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Audit Trails, Logs and Accountability',
                'summary' => 'Make every privileged action traceable.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Audit trails are the platform's memory of who did what. They protect users by deterring misuse, protect the platform by enabling investigation, and protect administrators by proving what happened. Making privileged actions traceable is a core governance duty.\n\nKnow what the platform logs: logins, permission changes, configuration edits, wallet operations, and support actions. Understand where logs go, how long they are retained, and who can read them. A log you cannot read when needed is not an audit trail; it is a filing cabinet.\n\nProtect the logs themselves. Audit logs should be tamper-evident and, ideally, stored outside the reach of the roles they audit. An administrator who can silently edit the logs defeats the entire purpose of having them.\n\nUse logs proactively. Review privileged actions periodically, not only after an incident. Patterns such as off-hours access, unusual data exports, or repeated permission changes are the early signals that something needs a question.\n\nFinally, act on what the logs show. When an audit reveals a mistake, fix the process; when it reveals intent, escalate. The organisation that reviews its audit trail, and is seen to review it, builds a culture where accountability is normal and misuse is rare.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Incident Response and Access Emergencies',
                'summary' => 'Contain, investigate, and recover from security incidents.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Every platform has incidents: compromised accounts, accidental configuration changes, data exposure, and abuse. Incident response is the rehearsed discipline that turns chaos into containment, investigation, and recovery. Super admins are the incident commanders.\n\nPrepare before you need it. Write the incident plan: the severity levels, the on-call path, the containment actions, and the communication duties. Rehearse the common scenarios, such as a compromised admin account or a misconfigured public page, so the first hour of a real incident is spent executing, not improvising.\n\nContain first. A compromised account gets its sessions revoked and its password reset; a dangerous configuration gets rolled back; an exposed dataset gets its access closed. Containment before diagnosis, because every minute of exposure compounds the damage.\n\nInvestigate with evidence. Preserve logs and records before cleaning up, establish the timeline, and determine scope: which accounts, which data, which users were affected. Facts before fixes; a fix that misses the root cause invites a repeat incident.\n\nFinally, communicate and learn. Notify affected users and, where required, the authorities, honestly and promptly. Then run the post-incident review and implement its actions. The platform that responds well survives incidents intact; the one that improvises does not.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Governance Metrics and Review Cadence',
                'summary' => 'Measure governance, and keep it alive over time.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Governance decays without attention. Roles creep, reviews slip, and logs go unread. Governance metrics and a review cadence are how a super admin keeps the control environment alive and proves it works.\n\nChoose a small set of governance KPIs: access review completion, privileged account count, two-factor adoption among privileged users, incident response time, change log completeness, and audit findings closed on time. Fewer, meaningful metrics beat a dashboard nobody reads.\n\nSet the cadence: monthly for operational metrics such as incidents and changes, quarterly for access reviews and role model review, annually for the full governance review and policy refresh. Put the reviews on the calendar now, because a governance review that happens only after an incident is an admission, not a control.\n\nReport governance to the right level. Executives need to know the platform's risk posture and the trends; teams need their action items. A short, honest governance report each quarter keeps leadership informed and reinforces the culture.\n\nFinally, treat governance as improvement, not compliance theatre. Each review should produce at least one concrete change: a tightened role, a retired permission, a better runbook. The platform whose governance improves every quarter is the platform that earns and keeps its users' trust.",
            ],
        ],
        'materials' => [
            ['title' => 'Super Admin Mandate Charter', 'type' => 'guide', 'notes' => 'Scope, separation of duties, and documentation duties.'],
            ['title' => 'RBAC Role Dictionary', 'type' => 'template', 'notes' => 'Roles, permissions, boundaries, and review dates.'],
            ['title' => 'Change Control Record', 'type' => 'template', 'notes' => 'Change, risk, rollback plan, window, and approver.'],
            ['title' => 'Incident Response Runbook', 'type' => 'guide', 'notes' => 'Severities, containment steps, evidence, and communication.'],
        ],
        'assessment' => [
            'title' => 'Super Admin Governance and RBAC Assessment',
            'instructions' => 'Answer all questions. A score of 70% or higher is required to pass. You have up to 3 attempts.',
            'pass_score' => 70,
            'questions' => [
                ['question' => 'What is the core principle of RBAC design?', 'a' => 'Give everyone broad access to avoid work', 'b' => 'Least privilege: each role gets exactly what its work needs', 'c' => 'One shared admin account for simplicity', 'd' => 'Permissions based on seniority rather than function', 'correct' => 'B'],
                ['question' => 'Why should shared or generic accounts be avoided?', 'a' => 'They are more expensive to license', 'b' => 'They destroy accountability because actions cannot be traced to a person', 'c' => 'They slow down login', 'd' => 'They are banned by all browsers', 'correct' => 'B'],
                ['question' => 'What is the first action in incident response?', 'a' => 'Write the post-incident report', 'b' => 'Contain the incident, such as revoking sessions or rolling back a change', 'c' => 'Notify every user immediately', 'd' => 'Delete the affected records', 'correct' => 'B'],
                ['question' => 'When should a departed employee\'s access be removed?', 'a' => 'Within a month, after the handover', 'b' => 'The same day they leave', 'c' => 'Only if they ask for it', 'd' => 'At the next quarterly review', 'correct' => 'B'],
                ['question' => 'What is the purpose of a change log?', 'a' => 'To make the admin team look busy', 'b' => 'To record who changed what, when, and why for review and recovery', 'c' => 'To replace the need for backups', 'd' => 'To hide changes from auditors', 'correct' => 'B'],
            ],
        ],
    ],

    15 => [
        'overview' => 'Wallet, Payments and Support Desk Operations trains the team that runs the platform\'s money and its customers. It covers payment operations and reconciliation, wallet ledgers and settlement, refunds and disputes, support desk triage, case ownership, fraud prevention, and service quality with SLAs and reporting.',
        'duration' => 420,
        'lessons' => [
            [
                'title' => 'Payment Operations and Reconciliation',
                'summary' => 'Keep every payment matched, from gateway to ledger.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Payment operations is the discipline of knowing exactly where every naira is: which payment partner received it, which wallet or order it belongs to, and what state it is in. Reconciliation is the daily habit that makes that knowledge true.\n\nUnderstand the payment flow end to end. A customer pays by card or bank transfer; the payment partner confirms; the platform credits the wallet or the order. At each step there is a reference, and the reference is the thread that ties the customer's money to their balance. Every operation must preserve it.\n\nReconcile daily. Compare the payment partner's settlement report against the platform's transactions: every successful payment should appear as a credit, every failed one as a failure, and any difference as an open item. Unmatched items are the queue where lost payments are found, so work the queue, do not just file it.\n\nHandle the common cases cleanly. Unreferenced bank transfers sit in a suspense account until matched; duplicate checkout attempts create multiple holds that release automatically; timed-out payments show as failed while the customer's bank may still show a debit. Each case has a defined resolution path, and knowing them prevents most payment tickets.\n\nFinally, keep the reconciliation evidence. A daily reconciliation record, with totals and open items, is the proof the finance team and auditors need. Payment operations done well is invisible to customers and unshakeable under audit.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Wallet Ledger, Holds and Settlement',
                'summary' => 'Run a ledger that balances and explains itself.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "The wallet ledger is the platform's bookkeeping for customer funds: every credit, hold, and debit recorded with a reference, a timestamp, and a reason. Running it well means the ledger always balances and any customer can be told exactly where their money went.\n\nUnderstand the ledger's states. Available funds are spendable; held funds are reserved for a pending transaction and release automatically when it completes or is cancelled. The distinction explains most balance questions: a customer seeing a smaller available balance is usually looking at a hold, not a loss.\n\nFollow double-entry discipline. Every movement has a source and a destination, so the ledger always balances. A wallet credit without a matching payment record, or a debit without a matching order, is an exception to investigate immediately, because unbalanced ledgers hide losses.\n\nManage settlement timing. Wallet credits from card payments are near-instant; bank transfers can take up to a business day when unreferenced. Know the timing for each method, and set customer expectations accordingly; a clear 'transfers match within one business day' prevents a flood of tickets.\n\nFinally, test the ledger's integrity. Run regular checks: the sum of wallet balances against the settlement account, holds against open orders, and refunds against their original payments. The ledger that is provably balanced is the ledger the support team can defend, line by line.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Refunds, Chargebacks and Disputes',
                'summary' => 'Resolve money-back cases fairly and by policy.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Refunds, chargebacks, and disputes are where money meets trust. Handling them by policy, with evidence, and at speed protects the customer, the counterparty, and the platform's integrity.\n\nStart with the refund policy and know it cold: which cases qualify, the evidence required, the approval levels, and the timelines. Refunds to wallet are immediate; refunds to card take several business days through the payment partner. Setting that expectation upfront prevents most follow-up tickets.\n\nInvestigate with evidence before paying out. Match the refund request to the order, the dispatch photos, the delivery confirmation, and the message history. A supported claim is settled fast; an unsupported one is declined with a clear explanation. Evidence-based decisions are fair to both sides and deter fraudulent claims.\n\nHandle chargebacks carefully. A chargeback is the customer's bank reversing a payment; respond with the evidence package within the partner's deadline, or the funds are lost by default. Track chargeback rates, because a rising rate threatens the platform's standing with the payment partners.\n\nFinally, learn from the pattern. Refunds cluster around causes: a fragile product, a slow seller, a confusing listing. The operations team that reports refund causes to the marketplace and product teams is the team that reduces refunds at the source, rather than just processing them faster.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Support Desk Operations and Triage',
                'summary' => 'Turn a queue of tickets into a system that works.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "The support desk is the platform's nervous system: every payment question, account problem, and complaint arrives here. Support operations is the discipline of triaging, routing, and resolving that queue so the right case reaches the right team fast.\n\nTriage every ticket on arrival. Classify by type, payment, account, order, verification, or technical; by severity, with money-down and safety cases first; and by route, because some cases belong to finance, some to verification, and some to the marketplace team. Triage is what stops urgent cases from drowning in a queue.\n\nUse the right tools for the job. Macros and templates for the common cases, escalation paths for the rare ones, and a knowledge base so customers can self-serve the frequent questions. A good knowledge base deflects a third of all tickets before they are ever written.\n\nOwn the case until it closes. Even when a case is routed elsewhere, the desk tracks it: is the customer informed, is the owner working, is the deadline met? Tickets that fall between teams are the classic service failure, and the tracking discipline is its cure.\n\nFinally, measure the queue: volume by type, first-response time, resolution time, and reopen rate. The numbers show where the process breaks, which templates to write, and which product is generating the most pain. The desk that measures improves; the desk that just answers stays busy forever.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Customer Communication and Case Ownership',
                'summary' => 'Communicate clearly and own the case to the end.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Customers judge support by communication: the clarity of the answer, the speed of the reply, and the feeling that someone owns their problem until it is solved. Case ownership is the discipline that delivers all three.\n\nWrite answers that resolve. State what you found, what happens next, and when the customer should expect it. Avoid internal jargon and reference numbers without explanation; a transaction reference is useful only when the customer knows where to find it. A clear answer today beats a perfect answer next week.\n\nSet and keep timelines. If a bank transfer needs a business day to match, say so and mark the case to follow up. When a timeline slips, tell the customer before they ask. The worst service experience is silence; the best is a proactive update, even when the update is 'still working on it'.\n\nOwn the case across handoffs. When a case moves to finance or verification, tell the customer who now owns it and when to expect contact. The handoff is where cases are most often lost, so close the loop: the customer should never have to re-explain their problem to the next team.\n\nFinally, close with confirmation. Before closing, confirm the customer's issue is actually resolved, not just that the ticket is answered. The support team that closes cases only when the customer agrees builds the kind of trust that turns a support contact into a loyal user.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Fraud Prevention and Risk Controls',
                'summary' => 'Spot fraud patterns and stop them at the gate.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Payments and wallets attract fraud: stolen cards, fake identities, social engineering, and refund abuse. Fraud prevention is the discipline of layering controls so that fraud is caught at the gate, not discovered in the reconciliation.\n\nVerify identity at the sensitive points. Account creation, wallet funding, and profile changes are where fraudsters concentrate. One-time passwords, document checks, and device signals are the layers that make fake accounts and account takeovers expensive and slow.\n\nWatch for the patterns. Multiple failed payment attempts, a new device plus an immediate profile change, an address that does not match the card, a rush refund request on a high-value order: each is a signal, and two signals together justify a step-up check. Teach the team the pattern list; it is the fraud defence that never sleeps.\n\nProtect the social engineering surface. Staff will be pressured: a caller claiming to be a coordinator, a grower in a panic demanding a refund, an 'urgent' transfer request. The control is process: no payment action without the ticket, the evidence, and the policy. The person who holds the line on process is the control.\n\nFinally, measure and adapt. Track fraud rates by type, the value caught at each control, and the false-positive cost to honest customers. Prevention that blocks everyone is as bad as prevention that blocks no one; the balance is found in the data.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Service Quality, SLAs and Reporting',
                'summary' => 'Define service promises and keep them measurably.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Service quality is what the support and payments function is judged on, and service-level agreements make it measurable. An SLA is a promise: respond within X, resolve within Y. The discipline is in keeping the promise and reporting the truth about when it was kept.\n\nDefine SLAs by case type and severity. Money-down cases get the fastest response; informational questions can wait longer. Publish the targets to the team so everyone knows the promise they are making, and surface them in the dashboard so performance against them is visible every day.\n\nStaff and schedule to the SLA. Ticket volume has peaks, after paydays, harvests, and program launches. A team sized for the average is a team that fails at the peak; schedule coverage around the calendar, and use the queue data to plan it.\n\nReport honestly. Weekly, report volume, first-response time, resolution time, reopen rate, and the SLA attainment percentage. A report that hides a miss to look good only delays the fix; the honest miss is the one that gets resourced.\n\nFinally, close the loop with root causes. The tickets that arrive repeatedly point at product or process problems upstream. A weekly review that sends the top ticket drivers to the owning teams turns support from a cost centre into the platform's early-warning system.",
            ],
        ],
        'materials' => [
            ['title' => 'Daily Reconciliation Sheet', 'type' => 'template', 'notes' => 'Gateway report vs platform transactions, open items queue.'],
            ['title' => 'Wallet Ledger Guide', 'type' => 'guide', 'notes' => 'Available vs held, double-entry discipline, settlement timing.'],
            ['title' => 'Refund and Chargeback Playbook', 'type' => 'guide', 'notes' => 'Policy, evidence pack, timelines, and escalation.'],
            ['title' => 'Support SLA Dashboard', 'type' => 'template', 'notes' => 'Volume, first response, resolution, reopen, SLA attainment.'],
        ],
        'assessment' => [
            'title' => 'Wallet, Payments and Support Desk Assessment',
            'instructions' => 'Answer all questions. A score of 70% or higher is required to pass. You have up to 3 attempts.',
            'pass_score' => 70,
            'questions' => [
                ['question' => 'What is the purpose of daily payment reconciliation?', 'a' => 'To create paperwork for auditors', 'b' => 'To match every payment from the gateway to the platform ledger and find open items', 'c' => 'To raise wallet fees automatically', 'd' => 'To count how many customers paid', 'correct' => 'B'],
                ['question' => 'A customer sees a smaller available balance than expected. What is the most likely cause?', 'a' => 'The platform stole the funds', 'b' => 'A hold reserved for a pending transaction', 'c' => 'A bank error that requires a branch visit', 'd' => 'Their password is wrong', 'correct' => 'B'],
                ['question' => 'Why should refunds be investigated with evidence before paying?', 'a' => 'To delay customers as long as possible', 'b' => 'To settle supported claims fast and decline unsupported ones fairly', 'c' => 'Because the payment partner requires a letter', 'd' => 'To train the support team in paperwork', 'correct' => 'B'],
                ['question' => 'What is the classic service failure at case handoff?', 'a' => 'Cases are answered too quickly', 'b' => 'Cases falling between teams with no owner', 'c' => 'Templates that are too clear', 'd' => 'Customers who read the knowledge base', 'correct' => 'B'],
                ['question' => 'What is the best fraud defence for staff facing pressure?', 'a' => 'Trusting callers who sound urgent', 'b' => 'Process: no payment action without the ticket, evidence, and policy', 'c' => 'Asking the caller for their birthday', 'd' => 'Speed, so the caller hangs up quickly', 'correct' => 'B'],
            ],
        ],
    ],
];
